      {
        "review_id": 130032250,
        "review_link": "https://www.tripadvisor.com/ShowUserReviews-g293890-d310712-r130032250-Pashupatinath_Temple-Kathmandu_Kathmandu_Valley_Bagmati_Zone_Central_Region.html",
        "title": "Extreme visit experience",
        "text": "This visit to the Pashupatinath hindu temple proved to be an extreme experience. Non-hindus are not allowed inside the inner art of the temple but can visit the rest if the grounds that have plenty to offer. Especially the crematoria along the very dirty river was an unique and new sight. Every hour or so a body was brought to the river banks were in the presence of family and friends the body was purified puting the feet of the deceased in the water. Also family and friends washed themselves with some hands full of river water. After that the body was placed on a wooden structure, carried to the nearby crematoria platforms, also on the river banks, placed on a stack of wood, and after a short ceremony, more wood and straw was placed on top of the body and set on fire. In the mean time the remains of other fires that had done most of their corpse burning were shoved into the river, the crematoria platform splashed with several buckets of river water until all washed down into the river and ready for the next corpse burning. Add to that the general rubbish and cows that make the river their home, while poor children try to find still something valuable in the river. You have to see the filth of the river to understand that after this unique experience we were also dying, but more for a good shower to wash ourselves mentally and physically clean. Still, highly recommended as a new experience and reference.",
        "review_tip": null,
